From 1535399de774b9a1065d42cfe9f1eccdfdf4a454 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:15:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 39/61] linux-widevine

Widevine in a Linux build presenting Windows (see the user-agent commit)
runs as Chrome's Widevine runs on Windows. Windows' CDM works in privacy
mode: a session opens with a service-certificate request (the two bytes 08
04) unless the page set a certificate on the MediaKeys, and the license
request that follows carries the client identification encrypted to the
license service's certificate, sized as Chrome 156's Widevine sizes it on
Windows. The Linux CDM sends its identification in the clear, naming Linux.
CdmAdapter runs Windows' flow over the Linux CDM
(media/cdm/windows_widevine.h). A request rebuilt this way no longer matches
the CDM's signature, so license servers refuse it: the major streaming
services (the list in windows_widevine.cc) and the sites
--widevine-playback-sites names get the Linux CDM unchanged and play.
getStatusForPolicy() answers as Chrome on a Windows desktop with an HDCP 2.2
display. Nothing changes on Windows or with --use-chromium-defaults. The
switch declaration rides in switch-resolver.
